--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Verification method for the copyleft claim
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The statement "this dependency tree contains no strong copyleft" was re-checked
for this file, and it is qualified rather than clean. Two passes were run over
the installed tree at node_modules/ (1,450 package.json files, four levels deep,
including nested node_modules):

  1. Declared metadata. Every package.json "license" / "licenses" field was
     collected. No AGPL, GPL, SSPL or BUSL declaration was found. One
     LGPL-3.0-or-later was found (@img/sharp-libvips-darwin-arm64, dev-only —
     see above).

  2. Licence file text. Every LICENSE / LICENCE / COPYING / NOTICE file was
     read and matched against the AGPL, GPL, SSPL and BUSL headers. Three files
     matched:

       - lightningcss/LICENSE and lightningcss-darwin-arm64/LICENSE — false
         positives. Both are MPL-2.0; the match is on MPL-2.0's own definition
         of "Secondary License", which names the GPL, LGPL and AGPL.
       - @mercuryworkshop/scramjet/LICENSE — a genuine AGPL-3.0 text, in a
         package that declares MIT. That package has since been removed; see
         the top of this file.

Pass 1 alone reports a clean tree. Pass 2 is what found the scramjet conflict.
Any future licence audit of this repository must read file text, not fields.

Both passes read node_modules, so both would have MISSED the third signal that
mattered here: the upstream repository carried no LICENSE file at all, which no
amount of reading the published tarball reveals. A dependency that ships a
licence its own repository never granted is worth checking at the source.
